{
  /*
   * Every Common test file, as one program. `npm run compile-tests`, run by
   * the compile-common job in the Compile workflow, type-checks it in parts
   * that extend this file and change only what they include
   * (tsconfig.tests.app.json, tsconfig.tests.ui.json and
   * tsconfig.tests.rest.json): as one program it outgrew an 8 GiB heap.
   * Scripts/compile-tests.js explains the split and checks that the parts
   * leave no file of this one out.
   *
   * Common's jest config transpiles only (ts-jest isolatedModules), the way
   * App's does, so the Common Test workflow does not type-check anything.
   * This is where that happens instead: once per commit, rather than once per
   * jest worker per import graph, which is what used to dominate every Common
   * Test shard.
   *
   * The build config (./tsconfig.json) excludes Tests so `npm run compile`
   * stays self-contained; everything else - paths, strictness, types - is
   * inherited, so a test is held to the same rules as the code it tests.
   * Common's global declaration files are listed as well: the build picks
   * them up through its default include, and this project would otherwise
   * only see what the tests import.
   *
   * Editors and ESLint's typed linting (projectService in eslint.config.js)
   * give each file the nearest tsconfig.json that includes it, and get the
   * parts rather than this program: Tests/App/tsconfig.json,
   * Tests/UI/tsconfig.json and Tests/tsconfig.json each extend one.
   * Scripts/Lint/LintByProject.js lints each program in an ESLint process of
   * its own, and this one as a whole ran out of that process's 12 GB heap on
   * 2026-10-27 (Tests/Scripts/LintTestPrograms.test.ts keeps the lint
   * projects to the parts).
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": [
    "Tests/**/*.ts",
    "Tests/**/*.tsx",
    "Typings/**/*.d.ts",
    "UI/index.d.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "build"]
}
